\newcommand{\BIPreface}{%
  \chapter*{Preface}\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Preface}\markboth{Preface}{Preface}
  A business begins with a choice. Somebody sees a problem, imagines a better
  outcome, and decides to do something about it. What follows is rarely as tidy
  as the plan. Customers surprise us. The economics change. A solution that
  worked for ten people becomes unreliable for a hundred.

  \medskip\noindent\SeriesTitle\ is being written to make those choices easier
  to understand. Its aim is to explain the mechanisms behind a business: why an
  opportunity is attractive, how an offering becomes useful, where the money
  comes from, and what allows an organisation to endure. A useful explanation
  should leave you able to make a better decision, in a situation different
  from the one on the page.

  \medskip\noindent Each chapter is a lesson. We begin with a concrete puzzle,
  develop the idea in ordinary language, and use mathematics or data where it
  makes the reasoning clearer. The lesson closes by following three fictional
  founders in technology, fashion, and education. Their different constraints
  show where the same idea travels well and where judgment must take over.

  \medskip\noindent This development edition establishes the reading design
  and chapter sequence. The prose, evidence, and cases will be developed chapter
  by chapter. This preface is a working editorial introduction for that process.

  \vspace{18pt}\noindent\textbf{\BookAuthor}\\Bayes Institute}
\newcommand{\BIFrontMatter}{%
  \hypersetup{pdftitle={\SeriesTitle: \BookTitle},pdfauthor={\BookAuthor},pdfsubject={Book \BookNumber\ of five}}%
  \frontmatter\BIInteriorTitle\BICopyright\BIPreface
  \chapter*{A way into this book}\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{A way into this book}
  \markboth{A way into this book}{A way into this book}
  \BookScope\par\medskip
  \noindent\textbf{The question at the centre}\par\noindent\BookQuestion\par
  \medskip\noindent Read the argument first. Pause at a figure or equation to
  ask what it changes about the decision. Then try the founder cases before
  reading their proposed moves. A box marks a definition, caution, or useful
  question; it does not replace the connected explanation around it.
  \section*{Five books, five decisions}
  Each volume stands on its own. Begin with the problem you face; the sequence
  also offers a route from an initial opportunity to an enduring institution.
  \begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,itemsep=5pt]
  \item \textbf{The Opportunity.} Choosing a worthwhile business.
  \item \textbf{The Offering.} Designing and delivering the promise.
  \item \textbf{The Engine.} Earning, surviving, and growing.
  \item \textbf{The Advantage.} Building capabilities that endure.
  \item \textbf{The Institution.} Allocating capital and outlasting the founder.
  \end{enumerate}
  \tableofcontents\mainmatter}
